    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101">

                <pb facs="0221" n="127" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg127"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Mr. Eustis.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-05-28"><hi rendition="#italic">May 28,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 676.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose for your information copy of a
                    dispatch No. 139, of the 31st of March last, from the consul of the United
                    States at Tamatave in regard to affairs in Madagascar.</p>
                <p>You will observe that the French resident-general, in his note of the 13th of
                    March last to Mr. Wetter, states, in effect, that France having taken possession
                    of the island, it follows from this fact that foreigners and French citizens are
                    alike amenable to the regular French tribunals, and that France has taken
                    definite and entire possession of Madagascar.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney.</hi></signed></closer>




